   {
    "text": "Figure 1.1. The movement from referral question to responsible decision. Each step constrains the next; the final decision inherits the uncertainties recorded at every earlier step.",
    "page": "3",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 2.1. The structure of a diagnostic interview. Each element supplies evidence for a different claim, and the mental status examination is interpreted against, not instead of, the history.",
    "page": "27",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 3.1. The psychometric chain. Each link is a separate empirical claim, and no link can be inferred from the strength of the others.",
    "page": "51",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 3.2. Positive and negative predictive values for an instrument with sensitivity .90 and specificity .90, plotted against prevalence. The values are computed from Bayes' theorem; the instrument is held constant while the setting varies.",
    "page": "63",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 4.1. The test-use cycle. Selection, administration and scoring each protect or threaten the validity of the interpretation that follows.",
    "page": "73",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 5.1. Personality assessment integrates model, method and context. No single instrument supplies the construct; the assessor must construct it.",
    "page": "92",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 6.1. Cognitive assessment as a sequence of claims, each requiring its own evidence. Capacity, context and consequence are separate questions.",
    "page": "112",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 7.1. Functional assessment links capacity, demand and support. The decision concerns the fit between them, not the capacity alone.",
    "page": "131",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 8.1. Assessment across the internalising spectrum. Overlapping presentations require explicit discrimination rather than reliance on the most salient symptom.",
    "page": "150",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 9.1. Assessment of severe presentations. Each domain supplies evidence that may support or undermine the others, and no single domain is decisive.",
    "page": "169",
    "indent": 0,
    "size": 8.8
   },
   {
    "text": "Figure 10.1. Assessment of personality disorder. Severity, traits, development and context must converge before a diagnosis is warranted.",
    "page": "187",
